The recursive JavaScript function `FindElementsBySelector` can be implemented as follows:

```JavaScript
function FindElementsBySelector(node, selector) {
    let elements = [];

    function testElementClassOrId(element){
        return element.className == selector || element.id == selector;
    }

    (function recurse(currentNode) {
        if (testElementClassOrId(currentNode)) {
            elements.push(currentNode);
        }

        currentNode = currentNode.firstChild;

        while(currentNode){
            recurse(currentNode);
            currentNode = currentNode.nextSibling;
        }
    })(node);

    return elements;
}
```

Below is a simple C# method that meets your requirements. Note that this code uses Microsoft's `System.Data.SqlClient` for SQL connection and commands, `Dapper` library for mapping result set to custom objects and `MemoryCache` for caching. It assumes you have already defined the structure of your custom object (for example, 'MyCustomObject').

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Data;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Dapper;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory;
using System.Runtime.Caching;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;

public class DBHelper
{
    private static readonly MemoryCache _cache = new MemoryCache("DBHelper");
    private static readonly object _locker = new object();

    public async Task<List<MyCustomObject>> ExecuteStoredProcedureAsync(IDbConnection connection, List<SqlParameter> parameters, string storedProcName)
    {
        var key = $"{storedProcName}_{string.Join("_", parameters.Select(p => p.Value))}";

        if (_cache[key] is Task<List<MyCustomObject>> resultTask) 
            return await resultTask;

        lock(_locker) {
            if (_cache[key] is Task<List<MyCustomObject>>)
                return await (Task<List<MyCustomObject>>)_cache[key];

            var cachePolicy = new CacheItemPolicy();

            resultTask = ExecuteStoredProcedureAsyncInternal(connection, parameters, storedProcName);

            _cache.Set(key, resultTask, cachePolicy);
        }

        return await resultTask; 
    }

    private async Task<List<MyCustomObject>> ExecuteStoredProcedureAsyncInternal(IDbConnection connection, List<SqlParameter> parameters, string storedProcName)
    {
        using (var multi = await connection.QueryMultipleAsync(storedProcName, param: parameters))
        {
            var result = await multi.ReadAsync<MyCustomObject>();

            return result.ToList();
        }
    }
}
``` 

This method first checks if the result is already in cache (`_cache[key]`). If it exists, it simply returns the cached value asynchronously. Otherwise, it executes `ExecuteStoredProcedureAsyncInternal` to get the result and stores it into the cache. The lock ensures that only one thread at a time can attempt to read from or write to the cache in order to prevent race conditions.

