using System;
using System.Data;
using System.Data.SqlClient;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.Text;

public static void ExecuteQuery(string connectionString, string sqlTemplate, Dictionary<string, object> parameters)
{
    var watch = Stopwatch.StartNew();

    try
    {
        using (SqlConnection connection = new SqlConnection(connectionString))
        {
            connection.Open();

            using (SqlTransaction transaction = connection.BeginTransaction())
            {
                using (SqlCommand command = new SqlCommand(sqlTemplate, connection, transaction))
                {
                    foreach (var param in parameters)
                        command.Parameters.AddWithValue("@" + param.Key, param.Value);

                    var reader = command.ExecuteReader();
                    try
                    {
                        while (reader.Read())
                        {
                            // Process each row here
                        }
                    }
                    finally
                    {
                        reader.Close();
                    }
                }

                transaction.Commit();
            }
        }
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(ex);
    }

    watch.Stop();
    Console.WriteLine("Execution Time: " + watch.ElapsedMilliseconds + " ms");
}
```
This code dynamically generates and executes a SQL query with parameters based on the input dictionary. It validates each parameter to prevent SQL injection attacks by using the 'AddWithValue' method of SqlCommand, which automatically escapes any SQL reserved words and adds quotes where necessary. The transactions allow multiple changes to be made before all or none of them are applied if there is an error.

This code logs any exceptions that occur during execution and provides a stopwatch for measuring the time taken to execute the query. This can be useful in optimizing performance by ensuring the most efficient SQL queries are being used. However, it's important to remember that this kind of high-level optimization might not always lead to improved overall application performance, especially when considering network latency and database server load.

Also note that for large result sets, the code only processes one row at a time to prevent excessive memory usage. If you have a specific need to process all rows at once or if your query is expected to return very large amounts of data (e.g., more than can fit into memory), you might need to reconsider this approach or look for other solutions like using paging, streaming, or bulk copy operations instead.

