Here is a C# code snippet that attempts to add and start a new website with the specified directory path and site name in an IIS server. It checks for `web.config` file before adding the site, logs an error if absent. This method uses Microsoft.Web.Administration DLL which provides full programmatic access to IIS configuration data:

```C#
using System;
using System.IO;
using Microsoft.Web.Administration;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public async Task AddAndStartWebsite(string directoryPath, string siteName)
{
    try 
    {
        // Verify web.config file presence before adding the website
        if(!File.Exists(Path.Combine(directoryPath, "web.config"))) 
        {
            throw new Exception("The specified directory does not contain a web.config file.");
        }

        using (ServerManager iisManager = new ServerManager())
        {
            // Adding the website
            Site site = iisManager.Sites.Add(siteName, Path.Combine(directoryPath, "web.config"), 80);

            // Start the site
            site.Start();

            await Task.CompletedTask; // Async method should return a task object
        }
    }
    catch (Exception ex) 
    {
        // Log error
        Console.WriteLine("An error occurred: " + ex.Message);
    }
}
```

Remember to add `using Microsoft.Web.Administration;` at the beginning of your file and make sure you have added reference to `Microsoft.Web.Administration.dll` assembly in your project settings. This DLL is usually located in `C:\Windows\System32\inetsrv` directory on a Windows Server.

