Here's a high-level C# code example using System.IO.BinaryReader and BinaryFormatter for deserialization. For dependency injection, I'll use Microsoft's built-in DI container but you can replace it with any other one. Also, logging is done using log4net package which can be replaced by another preferred logger.

```C#
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

public class DataLoader : IDataLoader
{
    private readonly ILogger<DataLoader> _logger;

    public DataLoader(ILogger<DataLoader> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public T LoadAndDeserialize<T>(string filePath) where T : class
    {
        try
        {
            if (!File.Exists(filePath))
                throw new FileNotFoundException("Binary file not found", nameof(filePath));

            var binaryFormatter = new BinaryFormatter();

            using (var fileStream = new FileStream(filePath, FileMode.Open))
            {
                using (var binaryReader = new BinaryReader(fileStream))
                {
                    return (T)binaryFormatter.Deserialize(binaryReader.BaseStream);
                }
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (!HandleException(ex))
        {
            throw;
        }
    }

    private bool HandleException(Exception exception)
    {
        // log the error
        _logger.LogError(exception, "Error during deserialization");

        return true;
    }
}
```
In the above code:
1. IDataLoader is a sample interface for managing dependency injection and ease of testing.
2. LoadAndDeserialize<T> function is used to load data from binary file and deserialize it into an instance of type T where T must be a class as BinaryFormatter can only serialize classes.
3. HandleException method logs the exceptions that could occur during deserialization and returns true if exception is handled to prevent propagation upwards.
4. The logger object is used to log any errors, which in this case includes any exceptions thrown by the binary formatter while deserializing data from a file. 

This code assumes that the serialized file contains data of type T. If the actual content differs from T (for example, if the file was saved with an older version of the class), you'd get a SerializationException when calling Deserialize(). In this case, you could add checks or try/catch blocks to manage deserialization issues and provide feedback in your application.

For handling multiple concurrent requests, you might want to use asynchronous methods instead of synchronous ones (like `Read` and `Write`) used above. You can achieve concurrency by using Tasks with async-await mechanism. However, for a microservices architecture, this may not be necessary if the deserialization process is separate from other parts of the application, i.e., data processing doesn't conflict with other requests.

Remember to ensure that exceptions are handled properly in your code. Exceptions should not cause crashes and provide meaningful error messages. You might want to re-throw an exception only when you can handle it or add more context (like wrapping the original exception). In this case, any exception during deserialization is caught and logged without crashing the application.

