This implementation uses POSIX semaphores and POSIX threads which are standardized across most Unix-like operating systems (Linux, BSDs, etc). It does not consider any sort of timeouts or interprocess communication for semaphores as these are a bit more complex and have platform-specific behaviors.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h>

static pthread_mutex_t semLock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void semOp(sem_t* sem, int op) {
    if (op == 0) { // wait operation
        int res;
        do {
            pthread_mutex_lock(&semLock); // critical section
            res = sem_wait(sem); 
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&semLock);
            if (res != 0) sleep(1); // handle timeouts here, waiting one second before retrying
        } while (res != 0);
    } else { // post operation
        pthread_mutex_lock(&semLock); // critical section
        sem_post(sem); 
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&semLock);
    }
}

void* threadFunc1(void* arg) {
    sem_t* sem = (sem_t*)arg;
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        printf("Thread 1: Waiting on the semaphore\n");
        semOp(sem, 0); // wait operation
        printf("Thread 1: Posted to the semaphore\n");
    }
    return NULL;
}

void* threadFunc2(void* arg) {
    sem_t* sem = (sem_t*)arg;
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        sleep(1); // simulate delay in the other thread
        printf("Thread 2: Posting to the semaphore\n");
        semOp(sem, 1); // post operation
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    pthread_t tid1, tid2;
    sem_t sem;

    if (sem_init(&sem, 0, 1) == -1) { // initializing the semaphore with an initial value of 1
        perror("Semaphore initialization failed");
        return 1;
    }

    pthread_create(&tid1, NULL, threadFunc1, &sem);
    pthread_create(&tid2, NULL, threadFunc2, &sem);

    // waiting for the threads to finish execution
    pthread_join(tid1, NULL);
    pthread_join(tid2, NULL);

    if (sem_destroy(&sem) == -1) { // destroying semaphore after use
        perror("Semaphore destruction failed");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("All done!\n");

    return 0;
}
```

This code creates two threads that share the same POSIX semaphore. One thread waits for a signal on the semaphore and then posts to it, while the other simply posts to it after sleeping for a second. 

Please note: This is just an example and might not cover all your requirements completely as creating IPC safe semaphores or handling timeouts are complex topics with platform-specific behaviors. You may want to look into interprocess communication (like Unix domain sockets or System V message queues) for this. Also consider using higher level constructs like `std::condition_variable` and `std::mutex` in C++11 or later if you're not constrained to POSIX semaphores.

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.lang.reflect.InvocationTargetException;
import java.lang.reflect.Method;
import java.nio.ByteBuffer;
import java.nio.channels.*;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.logging.Level;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class RemoteTaskManager {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(RemoteTaskManager.class.getName());
    private Selector selector;
    private SocketChannel socketChannel;
    private CompletionService<ExecutionResult> completionService;
    private ExecutorService executorService;
    private BlockingQueue<Runnable> taskQueue;
    private ThirdPartyAPI thirdPartyApi;

    static class Task {
        String methodName;
        Object[] parameters;

        public Task(String methodName, Object... parameters) {
            this.methodName = methodName;
            this.parameters = parameters;
        }
    }

    static class ExecutionResult {
        long executionTime;
        boolean success;
        String errorMessage;
        Object result;

        public ExecutionResult(long executionTime, boolean success, String errorMessage, Object result) {
            this.executionTime = executionTime;
            this.success = success;
            this.errorMessage = errorMessage;
            this.result = result;
        }
    }

    public RemoteTaskManager(int poolSize, ThirdPartyAPI thirdPartyApi) throws IOException {
        executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(poolSize);
        completionService = new ExecutorCompletionService<>(executorService);
        taskQueue = new LinkedBlockingDeque<>();
        this.thirdPartyApi = thirdPartyApi;
    }

    public List<ExecutionResult> executeTasks(List<Task> tasks) {
        List<Future<ExecutionResult>> futures = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Task task : tasks) {
            Future<ExecutionResult> future = completionService.submit(() -> processTask(task));
            futures.add(future);
        }

        // Gather all results and send statistics to third-party API
        List<ExecutionResult> executionResults = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Future<ExecutionResult> future : futures) {
            try {
                ExecutionResult result = future.get();
                executionResults.add(result);
            } catch (InterruptedException | ExecutionException e) {
                logger.log(Level.SEVERE, "Failed to execute task", e);
            }
        }

        // Send statistics to third-party API
        sendStatisticsToThirdPartyAPI(executionResults);

        return executionResults;
    }

    private ExecutionResult processTask(Task task) {
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        boolean success = true;
        String errorMessage = null;
        Object result = null;

        try {
            Method method = getClass().getMethod(task.methodName, task.parameters.getClass());
            result = method.invoke(this, task.parameters);
        } catch (NoSuchMethodException e) {
            success = false;
            errorMessage = "No such method: " + task.methodName;
        } catch (IllegalAccessException | InvocationTargetException e) {
            success = false;
            errorMessage = "Error invoking method: " + task.methodName;
        }

        long executionTime = System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime;
        return new ExecutionResult(executionTime, success, errorMessage, result);
    }

    private void sendStatisticsToThirdPartyAPI(List<ExecutionResult> executionResults) {
        // Collect and send statistics to third-party API
        for (ExecutionResult result : executionResults) {
            thirdPartyApi.sendUsageStatistics(result);
        }
    }

    private void connectToRemoteServer() throws IOException {
        socketChannel = SocketChannel.open();
        socketChannel.configureBlocking(false);
        selector = Selector.open();
        socketChannel.register(selector, SelectionKey.OP_CONNECT | SelectionKey.OP_READ | SelectionKey.OP_WRITE);
    }
}
```

In this example code, we create a `RemoteTaskManager` class that can execute tasks asynchronously using NIO channels and load balancing. The tasks are represented by the `Task` class which contains the method name to be executed on the remote server and its parameters. The execution results are represented by the `ExecutionResult` class, containing information about the execution time, success/failure status, error message (if any), and the result of the task execution.

The `executeTasks()` method takes a list of tasks as input, submits each task to an ExecutorCompletionService for asynchronous processing, and gathers all results. These results are then sent back to the third-party API using the `sendStatisticsToThirdPartyAPI()` method.

The `processTask()` method is used internally by the ExecutorCompletionService for executing each task in a separate thread. It uses Java reflection to invoke the specified method with its parameters, measures the execution time and handles any exceptions that occur during the execution.

Finally, we have placeholder methods `connectToRemoteServer()` for establishing a connection to a remote server using NIO channels. In a real implementation, you would replace these placeholders with actual code to connect to your remote server and handle I/O operations with NIO channels.

