Here's a basic implementation for your requirements. However, please note that logging in Rust is usually done with the `log` crate rather than an external system. This example doesn't include logging because of this, instead it just prints out messages using `println`. Also, handling errors and edge cases is very simplified here as a real-world application would need to be much more robust:

```rust
use std::time::Instant;
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{Read, ErrorKind};
use std::process::Command;
use std::mem::size_of_val;
use lazy_static::lazy_static;

extern "C" { fn getrusage(who: i32, usage: *mut libc::rusage); }

#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct ExitStatusExt(i32);
impl ExitStatusExt {
    pub fn is_success(&self) -> bool { self.0 == 0 }
}

fn get_resident_set_size() -> i64 {
    unsafe { 
        let mut usage: libc::rusage = std::mem::zeroed();
        getrusage(libc::RUSAGE_SELF, &mut usage);
        usage.ru_maxrss as i64
    }
}

fn compile(path: &str) -> Result<(), ExitStatusExt> {
    let start = Instant::now();
    let rss_start = get_resident_set_size();

    println_log("Compilation started.");

    if File::open(path).map_err(|e| e.kind())? == Ok(()) { 
        Command::new("rustc")
            .arg(path)
            .status()
            .map_err(|e| ExitStatusExt(1))
    } else {
        println_log("Source file does not exist or path is invalid.");
        Err(ExitStatusExt(2))
    }

    let duration = start.elapsed();
    let rss_end = get_resident_set_size();

    println_log(&format_log("Compilation ended.", duration, (rss_end - rss_start)));
}
``` 

This is a quite simplified implementation and may not work as expected. The `getrusage` function in Rust requires the `libc` crate which isn't widely used because it's considered unsafe. A more idiomatic way to get the resident set size would be using the `std::mem::size_of_val` function on a vector of your compiled data, but this doesn't actually measure the memory usage specifically.

For the logging part, you might consider looking at the `log` crate or other logging frameworks in Rust. These are far more robust and flexible than simple print statements and can be easily integrated into an external system such as Elasticsearch, Logstash or fluentd. 

