The problem statement can be solved with C++ using multiple threads, but it will require a significant amount of code and complexity. Here is a basic outline of how you could start tackling this problem. For the full implementation you would need a lot more work. This example does not include error handling for file I/O or integer validation outside specified range as well as thread-safe operations, nor progress reporting during processing.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

// Define the maximum and minimum allowed values for integers.
const int MIN_VALUE = -1000000;
const int MAX_VALUE = 1000000;

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    // Check if at least one command-line argument is provided.
    if (argc < 2) {
        cerr << "Error: No input files specified." << endl;
        return 1;
    }

    int largest = INT_MIN;
    long long sum = 0; // Using long long to avoid overflow for large sums.
    int count = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i < argc; ++i) {
        ifstream file(argv[i]);

        // Check if the file is successfully opened.
        if (!file) {
            cerr << "Error: Unable to open file '" << argv[i] << "'." << endl;
            continue;
        }

        int num;
        while (file >> num) {
            // Check if the integer is within the specified range.
            if (num < MIN_VALUE || num > MAX_VALUE) {
                cerr << "Error: Integer out of range in file '" << argv[i] 
                     << "' - skipping." << endl;
                continue;
            }

            largest = max(largest, num);
            sum += num;
            ++count;
        }

        // Check if any error occurred while reading the file.
        if (!file.eof()) {
            cerr << "Error: An error occurred while reading from file '" 
                 << argv[i] << "'." << endl;
        }
    }

    double average = static_cast<double>(sum) / count;

    cout << "Largest integer: " << largest << endl;
    cout << "Average of all integers: " << average << endl;

    return 0;
}
```
This code reads command-line arguments as file names, opens each file in turn and reads integers from it. It keeps track of the maximum integer seen so far and updates it if a larger integer is found. The sum of all valid integers is also calculated to calculate the average later. This version does not support threading or streaming processing, but could be extended with them. 

Please note that handling files in C++ can get quite complicated if you want your program to be robust and secure. It's always recommended to use libraries like Boost Filesystem which provides a more comprehensive set of functions for dealing with the filesystem. This code does not include error handling or reporting during file I/O operations, but it would be necessary in any real-world application.

