Unfortunately, the creation of a semaphore and its deletion after usage is platform-specific. Here's an example in C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <unistd.h>

/**
 * Creates a new semaphore with the specified initial value and deletes it after use.
 * @param initialValue The initial value for the semaphore.
 */
void CreateAndUseSemaphore(int initialValue) {
    // Create a new named semaphore
    sem_t* sem = sem_open("/MySemaphore", O_CREAT | O_EXCL, 0644, initialValue);

    if (sem == SEM_FAILED) {
        perror("Failed to create semaphore");
        return;
    }

    printf("Semaphore created with initial value %d.\n", initialValue);

    // Use the semaphore...
    // For example, wait for it:
    if (sem_wait(sem) < 0) {
        perror("Failed to wait on semaphore");
    } else {
        printf("Semaphore waited successfully.\n");
    }

    // Close and unlink the semaphore
    if (sem_close(sem) < 0 || sem_unlink("/MySemaphore") < 0) {
        perror("Failed to close/unlink semaphore");
    } else {
        printf("Semaphore closed and deleted successfully.\n");
    }
}
```
This function will work on Unix-like systems that support POSIX semaphores (e.g., Linux, macOS). Windows does not have native support for named semaphores, so you might need to use a third-party library or create your own wrapper for such functionality. Please note, handling semaphores correctly is important as incorrect usage can lead to unexpected behavior and even deadlocks. Always ensure correct initialization and cleanup of semaphores.

