Here is one possible implementation of `processFileUploads` in Java using the ExecutorService for managing threads:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import org.apache.commons.logging.*;
import org.springframework.web.multipart.MultipartFile;

public class FileUploadService {
    private static final Log log = LogFactory.getLog(FileUploadService.class);

    // Configure thread pool size according to your application needs, 
    // you may want to make this configurable or dynamically sized based on the expected workload
    private ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

    public void processFileUploads(Queue<MultipartFile> uploadQueue, int maxConcurrency) {
        for (int i = 0; i < maxConcurrency; i++) {
            executor.execute(() -> {
                while (!uploadQueue.isEmpty()) {
                    MultipartFile file = uploadQueue.poll();

                    try {
                        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

                        log.info("Starting upload of " + file.getName());

                        // Handle the actual file upload here, this could be to a local storage, remote server or cloud service. 
                        byte[] bytes = file.getBytes();
                        Path path = Paths.get(file.getName());
                        Files.write(path, bytes);

                        long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
                        log.info("File " + file.getName() + " uploaded in " + 
                            (endTime - startTime) + " ms");
                    } catch (IOException e) {
                        // Log the exception and continue with other uploads if possible, 
                        // you could retry or notify the user, this depends on your requirements
                        log.error("Error processing file " + file.getName(), e);
                    } finally {
                        try {
                            file.getInputStream().close();
                        } catch (IOException e) {
                            log.warn("Could not close stream for file " + file.getName(), e);
                        }
                    }
                }
            });
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, a fixed thread pool is used to handle concurrency and asynchronously process the files. For each upload, the file bytes are retrieved from the MultipartFile object and written to a local file using Java's NIO API. If any exceptions occur during processing of a file, they are logged and the next file in the queue will be processed.

