response:
Here's the implementation of `getMatchingElements` function that meets all the requirements mentioned above. This function uses a recursive method for traversal, async/await to handle external API calls, and error handling mechanism to ensure graceful failures. 

```javascript
interface ElementMetadata {
    id: string;
    class: string;
    description: Promise<string>;
}

async function getMatchingElements(rootElement: HTMLElement, selector: string, filterFn?: (element: ElementMetadata) => boolean): Promise<HTMLElement[]> {
    let matchingElements = [];

    async function traverseNodeList(nodeList: NodeListOf<ChildNode> | HTMLCollection) {
        for (let i = 0; i < nodeList.length; i++) {
            const node = nodeList[i];

            if (node instanceof HTMLElement && (node.matches || HTMLElement.prototype.matches).call(node, selector)) {
                try {
                    const elementMetadata: ElementMetadata = {
                        id: node.id,
                        class: node.className,
                        description: externalAPI.fetchDescription(node.id) // hypothetical external API call
                    };

                    if (!filterFn || await filterFn(elementMetadata)) {
                        matchingElements.push(node);
                    }
                } catch (err) {
                    console.error('Error processing element', node, err);
                } 
            }

            if (node.hasChildNodes()) {
                await traverseNodeList(node.childNodes);
            }
        }
    }

    try {
        await traverseNodeList([rootElement]);
    } catch (err) {
        console.error('Error occurred during DOM tree traversal', err);
    }

    return matchingElements;
} 
```
This function starts by defining an empty array `matchingElements` to hold the matched elements. It then defines a recursive async function `traverseNodeList` which processes each node in the provided list. If a node is an instance of `HTMLElement` and matches the provided selector, it attempts to fetch its metadata from an external API using the `fetchDescription` hypothetical method. The fetched metadata can be used by the filter function if provided. Any errors during processing are caught and logged, without breaking the loop for other elements. 

The traverse starts with the root element passed as a parameter to the main function. If any error occurs during DOM tree traversal (like lack of permissions or unhandled exceptions), it is also caught and logged. The array of matched elements is returned once all nodes have been processed. Note that `matches` method was used instead of `querySelectorAll` for performance reasons, as the former is faster in modern browsers due to its native implementation.

