Here's a C++ solution that implements all your requirements. It uses forward lists which are efficient at inserting elements and iterating over them, and allows for O(1) operations in terms of time complexity. The `std::advance` function is used to move an iterator to the position index specified. Error checking and handling have been implemented using exceptions.

```cpp
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>
#include <fstream>

void insert_after_position(std::forward_list<std::string>& flist, const std::string& target, const std::string& toInsert, int pos = -1) {
    auto it = flist.begin();
    auto prevIt = flist.before_begin();

    while (it != flist.end()) {
        if (*it == target) {
            // Insert at the specified position if provided and valid, otherwise insert at the end
            int currentPos = 0;
            ++prevIt;
            if ((pos == -1 || pos <= currentPos) && it != flist.end()) {
                return flist.insert_after(it, toInsert);
            }
        }
        ++currentPos;
        ++prevIt;
        ++it;
    }

    // If the target string is not found at all or beyond the specified position index, insert at the end
    if (pos == -1 || pos >= std::distance(flist.begin(), it)) {
        flist.insert_after(prevIt, toInsert);
    }
}
```

The `readFromFile` function reads string pairs from a text file and calls the insert operation on each pair. It uses an input file stream for reading data from the file:

```cpp
void readFromFile(std::forward_list<std::string>& flist, const std::string& filename) {
    std::ifstream infile(filename);

    if (!infile.is_open()) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Failed to open file " + filename);
    }

    std::string target;
    std::string toInsert;

    while (infile >> target >> toInsert) {
        insert_after_position(flist, target, toInsert);
    }

    infile.close();
}
```

Please note that for large input lists and complex string matching scenarios, using different data structures or algorithms may offer better performance. Also, the code assumes that all inputs are well-behaved and do not contain leading whitespace characters, which can be handled in a more robust way if needed. 

