```js
class StreamManager {
  constructor() {
    this.streams = [];
    // Add other initializations here...
  }

  addStream(buffer) {
    if (!Buffer.isBuffer(buffer)) throw new Error('Input must be a Buffer');
    this.streams.push(buffer);
  }

  copyBytesFromStreams(byteCount, callback) {
    let copiedData = [];
    let remainingByteCount = byteCount;

    for (let i = 0; i < this.streams.length; i++) {
      const stream = this.streams[i];

      if (remainingByteCount > stream.length) {
        copiedData.push(stream);
        remainingByteCount -= stream.length;
      } else {
        let newBuffer = Buffer.alloc(remainingByteCount);
        stream.copy(newBuffer, 0, 0, remainingByteCount);
        copiedData.push(newBuffer);
        break;
      }
    }

    if (callback && typeof callback === 'function') {
      // The total length of the merged buffer could be passed as well.
      callback(null, Buffer.concat(copiedData));
    } else {
      return Buffer.concat(copiedData);
    }
  }

  // Other functions like data integrity check etc...
}
```

In this simplified example, the StreamManager class is responsible for managing multiple buffers (representing chunks of data) and copying a specific number of bytes from these streams. A callback function could be provided to handle the merged buffer asynchronously. In production code, error-handling should be more robust and the copy operation optimized for larger amounts of data and concurrent access. 

