Here's how you might implement such an Octree in C++. We assume that we have an external database class Database which has methods fetch_data() to retrieve data from a network latency issue and a node class Node with properties to hold children nodes, data capacity, feature size, etc. 

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <future>

class Node {
public:
    std::vector<Node*> children;
    int data_capacity;
    double feature_size;
};

class Database {
public:
    // Method to fetch initial node data from database. Implementation is not shown here.
    void fetch_data(int node_id, Node& node) {} 
};

// Function to initialize Octree data structure.
Node* InitOctree(Database& db, int feature_size, int leaf_node_count, int data_capacity) {

    // Allocate memory for the root node of the octree.
    Node* root = new Node();
    root->feature_size = feature_size;
    root->data_capacity = data_capacity;

    // Initialize children nodes. This can be done concurrently to optimize performance.
    int child_node_count = leaf_node_count / 8;

    for (int i = 0; i < child_node_count; ++i) {
        root->children.push_back(new Node());

        // Asynchronously fetch data from database to handle network latency issues.
        std::future<void> result = std::async([&db, i, &root](){
            try {
                db.fetch_data(i, *root->children[i]);
            } catch (std::exception& e) {
                // Handle case where data fetching fails or returns incomplete data.
                std::cerr << "Error: " << e.what() << '\n';
            }
        });
    }

    return root;
}
```
The function InitOctree dynamically allocates memory for the Octree nodes and concurrently fetches initial node data from an external database. The error handling ensures that incomplete or failed data fetching is handled correctly without crashing the application. 

However, this code does not handle edge cases, potential race conditions when running in a multi-threaded environment, or clean up of allocated memory upon failure. Also, the actual implementation of fetch_data would depend on how the database is implemented and what kind of data it provides. For example, if you have to connect to a remote database, network latency issues might be handled at the database level rather than within this function. 

In terms of scalability, given that each octree node can contain up to eight children, the number of total nodes in the tree is approximately 8^depth, where depth is proportional to feature_size/node_capacity (let's assume it's an integer division). For a million leaf nodes and optimal distribution among child nodes, you would need roughly 1250 levels (depth=1250), meaning that each node can theoretically contain up to 8^1250 elements. This might be impractical or even impossible depending on your system's capabilities.

