Here's a basic implementation of your requirement in Java using CompletableFuture for asynchronous processing and SLF4J for logging. 

```java
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class UserProfileFetcher {
    private static final Logger LOGGER = LoggerFactory.getLogger(UserProfileFetcher.class);

    private ApiClient apiClient;
    private CacheManager cacheManager;

    public UserProfileFetcher(ApiClient apiClient, CacheManager cacheManager) {
        this.apiClient = Objects.requireNonNull(apiClient);
        this.cacheManager = Objects.requireNonNull(cacheManager);
    }

    public List<CompletableFuture<User>> fetchProfiles(List<String> userIds, UserProfileSchema schema) {
        return userIds.stream()
                .filter(schema::isValid) // validate IDs before API call
                .map(userId -> {
                    try {
                        Optional<User> cachedUser = cacheManager.get(userId);
                        if (cachedUser.isPresent()) {
                            return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(cachedUser.get()); // return from cache
                        } else {
                            return apiClient.fetchProfileAsync(userId)
                                    .thenApply(profile -> {
                                        cacheManager.put(userId, profile); // put in cache
                                        return profile;
                                    });
                        }
                    } catch (Exception e) {
                        LOGGER.error("Error fetching profile for user ID {}", userId, e);
                        throw new RuntimeException(e);
                    }
                })
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }
}
```
This method first validates the user IDs against a predefined schema. It then checks if the profile is already cached; if not, it fetches the profile from the API and caches it before returning. If any errors occur during this process, they are logged using SLF4J and rethrown as RuntimeExceptions for easier handling.

Remember to close the resources in a finally block or use try-with-resources statement to ensure they get closed after usage:

