Here's an example of how you might structure this in C#, using Entity Framework Core and Dapper micro ORM, along with a simple ILoggingService and IPermissionCheckingService:

```C#
public interface ILoggingService { void LogError(Exception e); }
public interface IPermissionCheckingService { bool CheckPermissions(string userId, string query); }

public async Task ExecuteQueryAsync(DbConnection connection, 
                                    string commandTemplate, 
                                    Dictionary<string, object> parameters,
                                    ILoggingService logger,
                                    IPermissionCheckingService permissionChecker)
{
    // Check user permissions before executing the query.
    if (!permissionChecker.CheckPermissions(parameters["userId"].ToString(), commandTemplate)) 
        throw new UnauthorizedAccessException("User does not have permission to execute this query.");

    // Remove the 'userId' parameter from dictionary, as it is not needed for SQL query generation.
    parameters.Remove("userId");

    // Generate a dynamic SQL command string from the provided template and parameters.
    var paramNames = parameters.Keys.ToArray();
    var sqlCommand = $"{commandTemplate} {string.Join(", ", paramNames)}";

    using (var transaction = connection.BeginTransaction()) 
    {
        try 
        {
            // Execute the command with Dapper ORM, which maps query results to POCOs for you.
            var result = await connection.QueryAsync(sqlCommand, parameters, transaction: transaction);

            // Handle large result sets by implementing pagination.
            foreach (var page in result.Buffer(100)) 
                foreach (var item in page) Console.WriteLine(item);
        }
        catch (Exception e)
        {
            logger.LogError(e); 

            // Rollback the transaction if anything goes wrong.
            transaction.Rollback();
        }
    }
}
```
This is a very basic example and may need to be adjusted based on your specific needs, but it should serve as a good starting point for building a robust SQL query executor method. This also assumes that you have Entity Framework Core and Dapper installed in your project. 

Note: Be aware of potential SQL injection risks when using `connection.QueryAsync` or any other methods similar to this, it could lead to critical vulnerabilities if not handled properly. Always validate and sanitize inputs before passing them into a command string. Also always consider whether the use case requires such dynamic querying - static typing can often provide more efficient code and better tooling support in .NET.

Here is a very high-level overview of how you can write this function. This example uses AWS S3 SDK. Node.js is used for asynchronous processing to handle multiple simultaneous uploads and manage concurrency. The code doesn't cover all the functional requirements mentioned above, but it gives an idea about how you might approach such a task:

```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const request = require('request');
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');
const url = require('url');
const mime = require('mime-types');
const authenticateUser = require('./authenticateUser'); // Assume this exists and handles user authentication.

AWS.config.update({ region: 'us-west-2' });

// Initialize S3 client with your credentials
const s3 = new AWS.S3({ apiVersion: '2006-03-01' });

async function uploadFileFromUrl(fileUrl, userCredentials) {
  // Authenticate the user first
  const isAuthenticated = await authenticateUser(userCredentials);

  if (!isAuthenticated) {
    throw new Error('User not authenticated');
  }

  // Parse the URL to get filename and file extension
  const parsedUrl = url.parse(fileUrl, true);
  const filename = path.basename(parsedUrl.pathname);

  if (!mime.lookup(filename)) {
    throw new Error('Unsupported file type');
  }

  // Initialize a write stream for the temporary file and download from URL to it
  const tempFilePath = path.join(__dirname, 'temp', filename);
  const writeStream = fs.createWriteStream(tempFilePath);

  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    request({ uri: fileUrl })
      .pipe(writeStream)
      .on('error', (err) => {
        console.log('Error downloading the file', err);
        reject(err);
      })
      .on('close', () => {
        // Once downloaded, upload to AWS S3
        fs.readFile(tempFilePath, async (err, filedata) => {
          if (err) {
            console.log('Error reading the file', err);
            reject(err);
            return;
          }

          const params = {
            Bucket: 'your-bucket-name',
            Key: filename, 
            Body: filedata,
            ACL: 'public-read' // Make the file publicly accessible.
          };

          try {
            const uploadResult = await s3.upload(params).promise();

            resolve(uploadResult.Location); // Returns public URL of the uploaded file.
          } catch (err) {
            console.log('Error uploading to S3', err);
            reject(err);
          } finally {
            fs.unlinkSync(tempFilePath); // Delete temp file after successful upload.
          }
        });
      });
  });
}
```
This is a simplistic example and lacks many of the complexities you would need to handle in a real-world situation, such as error handling, concurrency management, retries, progress tracking, splitting large files for multi-part uploads, fallback mechanism for cloud storage services. You'd also want to consider adding more sophisticated logging and data validation/security measures based on your project requirements.

