Here is a basic outline of the PHP function to perform authentication using an OAuth 2.0 client. This example uses the League's OAuth2 client package and assumes you have installed it via composer in your project. This also doesn't include any database operations or user information storing/updating, caching or rate limiting mechanism for simplicity. These are crucial components that need to be added based on the application requirements and security constraints.

```php
<?php
use League\OAuth2\Client\Provider\AbstractProvider;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Session\Session;
require_once __DIR__ . '/vendor/autoload.php';

class OAuthAuthenticator {
    private $provider = null;

    public function __construct(AbstractProvider $provider) {
        $this->provider = $provider;
    }

    public function authenticate() {
        if (!isset($_GET['code'])) {
            $authUrl = $this->provider->getAuthorizationUrl();
            session_start();
            $_SESSION['oauth2state'] = $this->provider->getState();
            header('Location: ' . $authUrl);
            exit;
        } elseif (empty($_GET['state']) || (isset($_SESSION['oauth2state']) && $_GET['state'] !== $_SESSION['oauth2state'])) {
            unset($_SESSION['oauth2state']);
            exit('Invalid state');
        } else {
            $token = $this->provider->getAccessToken('authorization_code', ['code' => $_GET['code']]);
            try {
                $userDetails = $this->provider->getResourceOwner($token);
                return $userDetails;
            } catch (Exception $e) {
                exit('Something went wrong: ' . $e->getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, the `OAuthAuthenticator` class takes a League's OAuth2 client provider as an argument in its constructor. The `authenticate()` function is used to authenticate users. If no authorization code exists (which would indicate that the user has not been redirected back from the OAuth server), it initiates the authentication process by generating and storing a state token, then redirects the user to the provider's authorization URL.

If an authorization code is present but the state does not match (indicating a possible security issue or a man-in-the-middle attack), it halts the process with an error message. If everything checks out, the function attempts to get an access token and user details from the OAuth provider. Any exceptions thrown during this process are caught and reported back to the user in the form of an error message.

