Here's a Java class named `DynamicResourcePoolManager` that provides advanced functionality for managing a pool of dynamic resources such as database connections, with specific parameters and realistic operational constraints. 

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.function.*;
import java.sql.*;
import javax.sql.*;
import org.apache.commons.dbcp2.*;
import org.apache.commons.pool2.*;
import org.apache.commons.lang3.RandomStringUtils;

public class DynamicResourcePoolManager {
    private static final Map<ConnectionConfig, DataSource> poolCache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private static final Random random = new SecureRandom();

    public void initializeResourcePool(int minConnections, int maxConnections, long connectionTimeout, ConnectionConfigCallback configCallback) throws Exception {
        if (minConnections <= 0 || maxConnections < minConnections) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid minimum or maximum connections.");

        BasicDataSource dataSource = new BasicDataSource();
        ConnectionConfig originalConfig = getConnectionConfigFromDatabase(); // Fetch the connection configuration from the database.

        if (configCallback != null) {
            originalConfig = configCallback.configure(originalConfig);
        }

        try {
            dataSource.setDriverClassName(originalConfig.getDriverClassName());
            dataSource.setUrl(originalConfig.getUrl());
            dataSource.setUsername(originalConfig.getUser());
            dataSource.setPassword(originalConfig.getPassword());

            if (connectionTimeout > 0) {
                dataSource.setLoginTimeout((int) connectionTimeout);
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new Exception("Failed to initialize the resource pool: " + e.getMessage(), e);
        }

        // Set the initial size, max total and max idle connections on the data source.
        dataSource.setInitialSize(minConnections);
        dataSource.setMaxTotal(maxConnections);
        dataSource.setMaxIdle(maxConnections / 2);

        poolCache.putIfAbsent(originalConfig, dataSource);
    }

    public void initializeResourcePoolWithSeed(int minConnections, int maxConnections, long connectionTimeout, String seed) throws Exception {
        if (seed == null || seed.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid seed.");

        // Generate a unique configuration for each thread to enable consistent behavior across different runs.
        ConnectionConfig config = new ConnectionConfig(RandomStringUtils.randomAlphanumeric(20), RandomStringUtils.randomAlphanumeric(20));
        config.setSeed(seed);

        initializeResourcePool(minConnections, maxConnections, connectionTimeout, (original) -> {
            // Apply the unique configuration to each thread.
            original.applyConfig(config);
            return original;
        });
    }

    private ConnectionConfig getConnectionConfigFromDatabase() throws SQLException {
        // Fetch the connection configuration from a database or other external system here. 

        String driverClassName = ""; // fetched from DB
        String url = "";             // fetched from DB
        String user = "";           // fetched from DB
        String password = "";       // fetched from DB

        return new ConnectionConfig(driverClassName, url, user, password); 
    }
}
```
In this class, we have a `ConnectionConfig` class to store the configuration details for each connection. There's also a caching mechanism in place using a `ConcurrentHashMap` that stores the `BasicDataSource` (a DBCP2-based data source implementation) instances created based on the `ConnectionConfig` configurations. 

The `initializeResourcePoolWithSeed()` method can be used to set a unique seed for each thread's resource pool configuration, which enables consistent behavior across different runs.

Please note that this is just a basic example and doesn't handle all of the functionalities you asked for, such as connection health monitoring or more advanced error handling mechanisms. The actual implementation would depend on your exact requirements, such as how you fetch configurations from the database, what kind of callbacks are needed, etc.

