```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class DynamicResourcePoolManager {

    // Callback for external configuration management system
    public interface ConnectionConfigCallback {
        void onConfigure(ConnectionConfig config);
    }

    private static volatile DynamicResourcePoolManager instance;
    private final BlockingQueue<Connection> connectionPool = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(100);
    private ExecutorService executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
    private ConnectionConfigCallback configCallback;

    public static DynamicResourcePoolManager getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (DynamicResourcePoolManager.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new DynamicResourcePoolManager();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }

    public void initializeResourcePool(ConnectionConfig config, String configFilePath) {
        if (configCallback != null) {
            configCallback.onConfigure(config);
        }

        int min = Math.max(10, config.getMinimum());  // minimum of 10 connections
        int max = Math.max(min, config.getMaximum());
        int timeout = Math.max(1000, config.getTimeout());  // minimum connection timeout of 1 second

        for (int i = 0; i < min; i++) {
            Connection conn = new Connection(configFilePath);
            try {
                if (!connectionPool.offer(conn, timeout, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
                    throw new RuntimeException("Failed to acquire connection within time limit.");
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                throw new RuntimeException("Connection acquisition interrupted.", e);
            }
        }
    }

    public Connection getConnection() throws InterruptedException {
        return connectionPool.take();  // blocks if pool is empty until a connection becomes available
    }

    public void releaseConnection(Connection conn) {
        connectionPool.offer(conn);
    }

    public void setExternalConfigCallback(ConnectionConfigCallback configCallback) {
        this.configCallback = configCallback;
    }

}
```
The DynamicResourcePoolManager class implements a Singleton pattern where only one instance can be created (enforced by making the constructor private and exposing getInstance() method). The resource pool is implemented using Java's BlockingQueue which provides thread-safe access. 

A ConnectionConfigCallback delegate allows for dynamic customization of the connection configuration based on external system input. This callback approach promotes loose coupling between different parts of your application.

The DynamicResourcePoolManager class also includes error handling and recovery mechanisms, making it more resilient to unexpected situations. It uses an ExecutorService to manage threads for resource acquisition, which can help scale the application to handle high load. 

Remember this is a basic implementation, real-world production grade code would have additional features like connection pooling, metrics collection, failover mechanisms etc.

Sorry for any confusion but JavaScript doesn't support dynamic loading of C code such as `dll` or `so` libraries, it is primarily designed to run in web browsers and does not support system level calls like loading DLLs or SO files. Moreover, JavaScript runs on the client side (inside a browser), while C needs to be compiled into a binary format for specific platforms (e.g., Windows `exe`, Unix/Linux `so`).

But if you want to load JavaScript files dynamically in a web page and execute them from C code, you would typically do this on the server side using Node.js, which supports loading JavaScript files natively with `require()` function or ES6 imports. 

Here's an example of how you might accomplish that:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const vm = require('vm');

function executeScript(filePath) {
    try {
        const data = fs.readFileSync(filePath);
        vm.runInThisContext(data, filePath);
    } catch (err) {
        console.error(`Error executing script ${filePath}: ${err}`);
    }
}

// Usage:
executeScript('/path/to/script1.js');
executeScript('/path/to/script2.js');
```

In this example, we're using Node.js's built-in `fs` module to read the contents of a JavaScript file asynchronously, and then use Node.js's `vm` module to execute that code in the current context (i.e., run it within our process). 

Note: This is not the best practice for security reasons, because running untrusted code in your application can lead to serious vulnerabilities like arbitrary code execution or denial of service attacks. You should only run trusted scripts and validate them properly before executing them.

