Regarding handling circular dependencies, Node.js supports ES6 modules which are synchronous by default, and they can only require other modules at the top level of their code. However, if you really need to handle such cases you might have to use an asynchronous module system like `require-asyn`, but it comes with its own set of problems.

For concurrency control, a Promise based approach is usually better suited than callbacks because it can easily be chained and can also be used with async/await syntax in modern Node.js versions.

As for the asynchronous reduce function:
```javascript
function asyncReduce(array, initialValue, cb) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    let accumulator = initialValue;

    const processNextItem = index => {
      if (index === array.length) {
        resolve(accumulator);
      } else {
        cb(accumulator, array[index], index).then(result => {
          accumulator = result;
          setImmediate(processNextItem, index + 1); // or use process.nextTick() for immediate execution
        }).catch(reject);
      }
    };

    processNextItem(0);
  });
}
```
Please note that error handling is critical in asynchronous programming and should be done properly with try/catch blocks or passed to the Promise's reject callback. Always test your code thoroughly.

