3. Asynchronous Array Reduce:

You could use Promises combined with `Promise.all()` to run the reduction operations in parallel across multiple CPU cores, using a semaphore-like mechanism to limit concurrency:

```javascript
let availableWorkers = 4;  // or however many you have
let workers = [];
array.forEach(item => {
  if (workers.length >= availableWorkers) {
    Promise.all(workers).then(() => {
      workers = workers.filter(worker => !worker.isResolved);
      reduceItem(item);
    });
  } else {
    reduceItem(item);
  }
});
Promise.all(workers).then(() => console.log("All operations completed"));
```
In this pseudocode, `reduceItem()` is a function that returns a Promise for the result of reducing an item. You could use async/await syntax to make this code more readable:

```javascript
for(let item of array) {
  if (workers.length >= availableWorkers) {
    await Promise.all(workers);
    workers = workers.filter(worker => !worker.isResolved);
  }
  workers.push(reduceItem(item));
}
await Promise.all(workers);
console.log("All operations completed");
