Here is an example implementation of your requirements in C#, using .NET's built-in cryptography libraries and `ConcurrentDictionary` for thread safety and caching:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;

public class HashService
{
    private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<string, string> cache = new ConcurrentDictionary<string, string>();
    private static readonly ILogger logger = LoggerFactory.GetLogger(System.Reflection.MethodBase.GetCurrentMethod().DeclaringType);

    public string GetHash(string input)
    {
        try
        {
            if (cache.TryGetValue(input, out var hash)) 
                return hash;

            using (var sha256 = SHA256.Create())
            {
                // Compute hash and convert to string representation
                var bytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(input);
                var hashBytes = sha256.ComputeHash(bytes);

                StringBuilder builder = new StringBuilder();
                for (int i = 0; i < hashBytes.Length; i++)
                    builder.Append(hashBytes[i].ToString("x2"));

                hash = builder.ToString();
            }

            // Add the computed hash to cache for future use
            cache.TryAdd(input, hash);
            return hash;
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            logger.Error("An error occurred while computing the SHA-256 hash", ex);
            throw;
        }
    }
}
```
In this implementation, we use a `ConcurrentDictionary` to cache recently computed hashes. The `GetHash` method first checks if the input string is in the cache. If it is, it returns the cached value directly. If not, it computes the hash using SHA-256 and stores it in the cache before returning it.

We also use a logging system to log any errors that occur during computation of hashes. We catch exceptions and throw them after logging, so that they can be handled by the calling code if necessary.

This implementation assumes you have access to a `LoggerFactory` class with a method for creating an `ILogger` instance. You will need to replace this with your own logging framework or library of choice.

