This is a complex task that requires understanding of thread safety, memory management, DOM tree traversal, CSS selector handling and error handling. Below is a basic outline of how one might implement such function using POSIX threads (pthreads) in C. Please note that this code does not include fetching remote resources or implementing CSS selectors as these could be highly dependent on specific libraries or external APIs used for those operations. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct Node {
    // ...
} Node;

typedef struct ResultNode {
    void* data;
    struct ResultNode* next;
} ResultNode;

void* traverse(Node* root, int depth_limit, void* (*css_selector)(char*), void (*callback)(Node*, void**), pthread_mutex_t* mutex) {
    static ResultNode* results = NULL;

    if (depth_limit <= 0 || !root) return NULL;

    // Apply CSS selector
    void* result = css_selector((char*) root); 

    if (result) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(mutex);

        ResultNode* newResult = malloc(sizeof(ResultNode));
        newResult->data = result;
        newResult->next = results;
        results = newResult;

        pthread_mutex_unlock(mutex);
    }

    // Callback on current node
    callback(root, &result); 

    for (int i = 0; i < root->childrenCount; ++i) {
        traverse(root->children[i], depth_limit - 1, css_selector, callback, mutex);
    }

    return results;
}
```
This is a basic outline of the function. It recursively goes through each node in the DOM tree while applying the given CSS selector to each node and invoking the provided callback on it. If there are child nodes in this node, then it continues traversing them as well until depth limit is reached. This version doesn't use pthreads for parallel execution but could be adapted easily by creating a separate thread per node or chunk of work using pthreads.

Please note that the implementation of the CSS selector and callback function would depend on your specific requirements, and you might need to add more error handling code. Also, please remember to free any allocated memory when it's no longer needed to prevent memory leaks in a real application. 

