```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Objects;

public class DynamicResourcePoolManager {

    private static final AtomicInteger poolCounter = new AtomicInteger();
    private Map<String, Pool> pools = new HashMap<>();

    public String initializeResourcePool(int minConnections, int maxConnections, long connectionTimeout) {
        if (minConnections <= 0 || maxConnections <= 0 || minConnections > maxConnections) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid parameters for connections");
        }

        String poolId = "pool_" + poolCounter.incrementAndGet();
        Pool pool = new Pool(poolId, minConnections, maxConnections, connectionTimeout);
        pools.put(poolId, pool);
        return poolId;
    }

    private static class Pool {
        String id;
        int minConnections;
        int maxConnections;
        long connectionTimeout;

        public Pool(String id, int minConnections, int maxConnections, long connectionTimeout) {
            this.id = id;
            this.minConnections = minConnections;
            this.maxConnections = maxConnections;
            this.connectionTimeout = connectionTimeout;
        }

        @Override
        public boolean equals(Object o) {
            if (this == o) return true;
            if (!(o instanceof Pool)) return false;
            Pool pool = (Pool) o;
            return id.equals(pool.id);
        }

        @Override
        public int hashCode() {
            return Objects.hash(id);
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the `DynamicResourcePoolManager` class manages a collection of pools using a `HashMap<String, Pool>`. Each pool has an ID and can be customized with minimum and maximum number of connections along with a connection timeout. The `initializeResourcePool` method takes these parameters as inputs and creates a new pool with the specified configuration. It returns the ID of the created pool, which is used to reference it in later operations.

Please note that this class does not manage any actual resources or connections, it just manages the configurations for pools of such resources. In real-world scenarios, you would need additional classes and methods to handle connection creation, management, and cleanup as well as any error handling and recovery logic. For integration with external systems for customizing configuration, you could use a factory pattern where `ConnectionConfig` object is supplied by the client which in turn can be modified by callback mechanism.

