Here's how you might implement this in Rust. I will not be able to provide every piece of code as it would require a lot more context. However, here is a basic example of what you can do for inserting strings into a `Vec`. Please note that the full implementation would need more complex handling and additional libraries like 'SyncLazy', 'rustc_driver::catch_with_exit_code', 'early_error' etc.

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};

type SharedList = Arc<Mutex<Vec<String>>>;

fn insert_after(list: &SharedList, target: &str, replacement: &str) {
    let mut guard = list.lock().unwrap();

    if let Some(index) = guard.iter().position(|value| value == target) {
        // If found, insert after the first occurrence of `target`
        guard.insert(*index + 1, replacement.to_string());

    } else {
        // If not found, append at the end of the list
        guard.push(replacement.to_string());
    }
}
```

This function assumes that you have a shared mutable reference to a `Vec<String>` and inserts a string after the first occurrence of a given target string, or appends it at the end if not found. It's designed to be thread-safe by locking access to the list with a mutex so that multiple threads can safely modify it concurrently.

