Here is an abstract implementation of `DistributedCacheFileProcessor` in Java using Netty's zero-copy file read/write capabilities. For simplicity, I am assuming that ZooKeeper based distributed coordination services are out of scope for this problem and thus are not included here. 

```java
import io.netty.buffer.ByteBuf;
import io.netty.channel.ChannelHandlerContext;
import io.netty.handler.stream.ChunkedInput;
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.channels.FileLockInterruptionException;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.ScheduledExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class DistributedCacheFileProcessor {
    private final int maxBufferSize = 1024 * 1024 * 1024; // 1GB limit for heap usage
    private ScheduledExecutorService executor = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());

    public void processLargeFileAsync(String filePath, CustomProcessingFunction function) {
        executor.submit(() -> {
            try (RandomAccessFile raf = new RandomAccessFile(filePath, "r")) {
                FileLock lock = raf.getChannel().lock();
                ByteBuffer buffer = MappedByteBuffer.allocateDirect(maxBufferSize);
                long position = 0;
                while (position < raf.length()) {
                    int readBytes = raf.read(buffer);
                    if (readBytes > 0) {
                        function.process((ByteBuf) buffer, 0, readBytes);
                        position += readBytes;
                    }
                }
            } catch (IOException e) {
                // Error handling for IO exceptions
                e.printStackTrace();
            } catch (SecurityException e) {
                // handle security exception
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        });
    }

    private interface CustomProcessingFunction {
        void process(ByteBuf buffer, int offset, int length);
    }
}
```

The `processLargeFileAsync` method reads the file in chunks of size up to 1GB using a direct ByteBuffer for zero-copy read/write operations. The read bytes are passed to a custom processing function which should handle your logic. Error handling is done for checked exceptions like IOExceptions and SecurityException.

