Here's a very basic Java implementation using Google Cloud Storage API. Please note that this is a simplified solution for educational purposes only as handling large files, implementing different strategies for conflict resolution, managing resources, and many other aspects are left out of the scope of this task.

```java
import com.google.cloud.storage.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

public class SyncService {
    private static Storage storage = StorageOptions.getDefaultInstance().getService();
    private static String bucketName = "your-bucket";

    public static void sync(Path localDir, Path remoteDir) throws Exception {
        Files.walkFileTree(localDir, new SimpleFileVisitor<Path>() {
            @Override
            public FileVisitResult visitFile(Path file, BasicFileAttributes attrs)
                    throws Exception {
                String relativePath = localDir.relativize(file).toString();
                Path remoteFilePath = remoteDir.resolve(relativePath);

                if (Files.exists(remoteFilePath)) {
                    long localModifiedTime = Files.getLastModifiedTime(file).toMillis();
                    Blob blob = storage.get(bucketName, relativePath);
                    long remoteModifiedTime = blob.getUpdateTime();

                    if (localModifiedTime > remoteModifiedTime) {
                        // Upload the file to GCS
                        BlobId blobId = BlobId.of(bucketName, relativePath);
                        BlobInfo blobInfo = BlobInfo.newBuilder(blobId).build();
                        storage.create(blobInfo, Files.readAllBytes(file));
                    } else if (localModifiedTime < remoteModifiedTime) {
                        // Download the file from GCS
                        Files.write(remoteFilePath, storage.readAllBytes(bucketName, relativePath), StandardOpenOption.CREATE);
                    }
                } else {
                    // Upload the file to GCS
                    BlobId blobId = BlobId.of(bucketName, relativePath);
                    BlobInfo blobInfo = BlobInfo.newBuilder(blobId).build();
                    storage.create(blobInfo, Files.readAllBytes(file));
                }

                return FileVisitResult.CONTINUE;
            }
        });
    }
}
``` 
This class continuously synchronizes the local directory with a remote Google Cloud Storage bucket. It compares last modified timestamps of files in both locations to determine which is more recent and therefore needs updating on the other side. If one file doesn't exist, it simply uploads or downloads the missing file. Note that this code does not handle conflicts between two clients editing the same file.

The `sync` method could be called with a timer or another form of scheduling mechanism to continuously check and synchronize files. Also note that exceptions are ignored for simplicity. A real-world solution would need to properly handle these exceptions, possibly including retries and a suitable fallback strategy when operations fail due to network errors or service unavailability.

Here's a rough implementation of `fetchAndFilterDynamicContent` in NodeJS environment using Promises, Async/Await and Axios for http requests. For simplicity sake, it doesn't include rate limiting or API call management features but those can be added as necessary. Caching is also not implemented here:

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');
const asyncQueue = require('async/queue'); // install with npm if needed

let queue = asyncQueue((task, callback) => {
    task().then(callback);
}, 5); // Only allow 5 concurrent API calls at the same time

function fetchAndFilterDynamicContent(selector, arrayOfElements, numElems, fsa) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        const states = [];
        let elemIndex = 0;
        while (elemIndex < numElems && elemIndex < arrayOfElements.length) {
            queue.push(async () => {
                try {
                    // Fetch data from external APIs for current element
                    let res = await axios.get('external-api/url'); 

                    const metadata = res.data;
                    states.push({ elem: arrayOfElements[elemIndex], metadata });

                    if (states.length > 1) { // Update 'next' pointer of the last state in list
                        states[states.length - 2].next = states[states.length - 1];
                    }

                    return elemIndex + 1;
                } catch (err) {
                    console.error(`Error fetching metadata for element at index ${elemIndex}: ${err}`);
                    // Return the current index so it can be retried, if desired
                    return elemIndex; 
                }
            });
            ++elemIndex;
        }

        queue.drain = () => { resolve(states); };
    });
}
```

This implementation fetches data from external APIs in parallel for each element concurrently, up to a maximum of 5 simultaneous API calls (controlled by the asyncQueue). It uses Axios to make HTTP requests and handles errors accordingly. The function returns a Promise that resolves with the resulting list of states when all elements have been processed and their metadata fetched, or rejects if an error occurs during any of the API calls.

