The following C code provides an example of how such a mechanism might be implemented with a fixed window algorithm. This basic form does not take into account multiple request types, integrating with an external monitoring system or self-tuning capacity based on historical data as these require more extensive implementations and design decisions that are outside the scope of this question. 

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    pthread_mutex_t mutex;
    time_t startTime;
    int requestCount;
    int limitPerWindow;
    int windowSizeInSeconds;
} RateLimiter;

void initialize(RateLimiter *rl, int limit) {
    pthread_mutex_init(&(rl->mutex), NULL);
    rl->startTime = time(NULL);
    rl->requestCount = 0;
    rl->limitPerWindow = limit;
    rl->windowSizeInSeconds = 1;  // Fixed window size of 1 second.
}

int allowRequest(RateLimiter *rl) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&(rl->mutex));

    time_t currentTime = time(NULL);
    if (currentTime - rl->startTime >= rl->windowSizeInSeconds) {
        // Reset window.
        rl->startTime = currentTime;
        rl->requestCount = 0;
    }

    if (rl->requestCount < rl->limitPerWindow) {
        // Allow request and increment counter.
        rl->requestCount++;
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&(rl->mutex));
        return 1;
    } else {
        // Reject request.
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&(rl->mutex));
        return 0;
    }
}
```
The `initialize` function sets up a new RateLimiter with the provided limit per window and starts a new time window. The `allowRequest` function checks if a request is allowed based on the current rate and increments the counter if it is.

This implementation does not provide an external monitoring system, multiple types of requests or self-tuning capacity. Further enhancements could include:
